From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:55:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat(session): bound the number of retained peer records

The live-peer semaphore limits how many peers are connected at once, but
every distinct address learned from trackers, DHT, PEX, explicit input or
an incoming connection is inserted into the torrent's peer map (and the
peer-adder queue) first. Queued, backing-off, dead and not-needed records
hold no live-peer permit, so a long-running session can retain an
unbounded number of records even with peer_limit set.

Add optional limits on retained records:

 - SessionOptions::known_peer_limit (per torrent default) and
   AddTorrentOptions::known_peer_limit (per-add override);
 - SessionOptions::known_peer_limit_total (shared across the session).

None keeps today's unlimited behaviour; 0 rejects every new record. A
record takes a permit from each configured limit when it is inserted and
keeps it inside the Peer until the record leaves the map. Both new
outgoing addresses and new incoming addresses go through this admission
path; an existing record that reconnects does not need a second permit.
When the limit is full, a discovered address is ignored (as for a
duplicate) and an incoming connection gets the new
AddIncomingPeerResult::KnownPeerLimitReached.

Two small fixes ride along, because without them records outlive their
usefulness and the limit would fill up with dead entries:

 - a record created by an incoming connection (no outgoing address to
   retry) is removed when its connection ends, instead of lingering in
   Dead/NotNeeded forever;
 - reconnect_not_needed_peer now moves a NotNeeded record to Queued
   before returning it, also when its outgoing address differs from the
   record handle. Previously that case stayed NotNeeded, so every
   reconnect trigger enqueued the same peer again. The peer queue now
   carries (record handle, outgoing address) to keep the two apart.

The limits default to None and are read at session/torrent construction
only. The two fixes above apply whether or not a limit is set. Tests cover the per-torrent and shared ceilings,
release when a record is removed, release of the local slot when the
shared acquisition fails, and the alternate-address requeue.
